event: response.output_text.done
data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_000ef488c3c1c2bd016a68ba0213e08190ba7edefb66797390","logprobs":[],"output_index":1,"sequence_number":205,"text":"PASS\n\n**Overall belief-change assessment:** The chapter completes the assigned transition with exactly the same force as the real chapter because the two texts are identical. It specifically removes the belief that attraction to sweetness is a personal weakness: “Our love of sweet things should not be seen as a weakness. It is what helped us to survive for millions of years.” The squirrel and wild-animal questions let the reader infer that natural eating does not require willpower, while the distinction between naturally sweet foods and refined sugar reallocates blame to the impostor: BAD SUGAR “fools us into thinking they are tasty” and causes us to “act against our true instincts.” The chapter therefore leaves the reader seeing the instinct as trustworthy and the refined substance as deceptive, not seeing themselves as defective. It also delivers the assigned second instruction—“KEEP AN OPEN MIND”—and uses the visual illusion to prepare the reader to question sugar’s apparent pleasure without prematurely attempting the full benefits-demolition reserved for later chapters."}

